.TH "sudo" "1"
.SH "NAME"
.PP
sudo \- translate sudo commands to doas
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.PP
\fBsudo\fP [sudo flags] command [args]
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.PP
\fBsudo\fP translates a command line written for \fIsudo\fP into a \fIdoas\fP
command line and runs it. It is one applet of the \fIdoas\-common\fP multicall
binary and lives next to the \fIdoas\fP and \fIdoasedit\fP applets as a symbolic
link.
.PP
The \fIdoas\fP applet runs in the same process; the effective uid that the
setuid installation grants is dropped while the command line is translated and
raised again before \fIdoas\fP takes over, so the privileges in effect are the
same as when a separate \fIdoas\fP binary was executed.
.PP
Because the sudo feature set is larger than the doas one, most sudo commands
cannot be converted into an exact doas command. For example, sudo has a flag
\fB--preserve-env\fP with no doas equivalent, but the \fIdoas.conf\fP options
\fBkeepenv\fP and \fBsetenv\fP can produce a similar effect. The aim is to make
replacing \fIsudo\fP with \fIdoas\fP possible on systems whose scripts rely on
sudo specific behaviour: as many flags as possible are translated, the rest are
ignored and left to \fIdoas.conf\fP.
.PP
Flags that follow the argument \fB--\fP are passed to \fIdoas\fP unchanged.
.SH "FLAG TRANSLATION"
.PP
Each valid sudo flag is either ignored, translated to a doas flag, or causes
\fBsudo\fP to exit.
.TS
center,box;
l l l .
sudo	doas	action
_
\-A		IGNORE
\-\-askpass		IGNORE
\-B		IGNORE
\-\-bell		IGNORE
\-b	\-n	PASS
\-\-background	\-n	PASS
\-C		IGNORE
\-\-close\-from=		IGNORE
\-D		IGNORE
\-\-chdir=		IGNORE
\-E		IGNORE
\-\-preserve\-env		IGNORE
\-\-preserve\-env=		IGNORE
\-e		EXIT
\-\-edit		EXIT
\-g		IGNORE
\-\-group=		IGNORE
\-H		IGNORE
\-\-set\-home		IGNORE
\-\-help		IGNORE
\-h		IGNORE
\-\-host=		IGNORE
\-i	\-s	PASS
\-K		IGNORE
\-\-remove\-timestamp		IGNORE
\-k		IGNORE
\-\-reset\-timestamp		IGNORE
\-l		IGNORE
\-\-list		IGNORE
\-N		IGNORE
\-\-no\-update		IGNORE
\-n	\-n	PASS
\-\-non\-interactive	\-n	PASS
\-P		IGNORE
\-\-preserve\-groups		IGNORE
\-p		IGNORE
\-\-prompt=		IGNORE
\-R		IGNORE
\-\-chroot=		IGNORE
\-S		IGNORE
\-\-stdin		IGNORE
\-s	\-s	PASS
\-\-shell	\-s	PASS
\-U		IGNORE
\-\-other\-user=		IGNORE
\-T		IGNORE
\-\-command\-timeout=		IGNORE
\-u	\-u	PASS
\-\-user=	\-u	PASS
\-V		IGNORE
\-\-version		IGNORE
\-v		IGNORE
\-\-validate		IGNORE
.TE
.SH "ENVIRONMENT"
.PP
The variables \fISUDO_USER\fP, \fISUDO_UID\fP and \fISUDO_GID\fP are exported
before \fIdoas\fP runs. Scripts that rely on them need matching \fIdoas.conf\fP
rules, for example:
.RS
.nf
permit setenv { SUDO_USER SUDO_UID SUDO_GID } user as root
.fi
.RE
.PP
By default \fBsudo\fP echoes the received sudo command line and the translated
doas command line to standard error:
.RS
.nf
$ sudo whoami
sudo <<< sudo whoami
sudo >>> /usr/bin/doas whoami
root

.fi
.RE
.PP
Setting \fISUDO_HIDE\fP (to any value, including the empty string) disables this
trace.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.PP
The exit status of the command run through \fIdoas\fP, or 1 when the translation
leads to a \fIdoas\fP error. When no \fIdoas\fP binary is found in
\fIPATH\fP the applet prints a message and exits 255. The \fB-e\fP and
\fB--edit\fP flags exit successfully without running anything.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR doas (1),
.BR doas.conf (5),
.BR doasedit (1)
